<p>OpenAI has added a clearer way for teams to connect ChatGPT Work and Codex usage with business outcomes. The September 16, 2026 update combines usage and cost data, task insights and outcome metrics in the ChatGPT Admin Console. The useful part is not a single ROI score. It is the idea of starting with actual work, defining a baseline and measuring what changed.</p>
<figure class="article-image"><img src="/assets/images/blog/measure-ai-roi-chatgpt-business-value.svg" width="1200" height="630" alt="AI ROI measurement workflow connecting usage, workflow changes and business outcomes"></figure>
<h2>Why usage alone is not enough</h2>
<p>Prompt counts, active users and token usage can show adoption, but they do not explain whether a workflow improved. A team may use AI heavily without reducing delivery time, improving quality or changing a meaningful business metric.</p>
<p>OpenAI’s new Insights approach starts with usage and task data, then asks the business owner to define the outcome worth measuring. That creates a path from “people use AI” to “this workflow changed in a measurable way.”</p>
<h2>What the ChatGPT Admin Console now exposes</h2>
<p>OpenAI says the Usage view brings together active users, credits and token usage across ChatGPT Work and Codex. Administrators can filter usage by group or user to see where adoption is growing, where spend is concentrated and where a team may need help with training or workflow design.</p>
<p>The task classifier groups a sample of messages into use cases and tasks. Examples include software engineering, sales and revenue work, and other business activities. The Use cases view provides a more detailed breakdown of tasks, credits, messages and active users.</p>
<h2>Build the measurement around a baseline</h2>
<p>A practical AI ROI measurement starts before the change is made. Pick one workflow that matters to the business, record how it works today, then introduce the AI-assisted process.</p>
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What to document</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Baseline</td><td>Current time, quality and outcome</td></tr><tr><td>AI workflow</td><td>Where AI is used and what humans still review</td></tr><tr><td>Outcome</td><td>The business metric that should change</td></tr><tr><td>Review date</td><td>When the team will compare results</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>For SEO teams, that could mean content briefs, technical QA or reporting. For engineering, it could mean code review, testing or incident work. For sales, it could mean account research or proposal preparation.</p>
<h2>Separate productivity from business value</h2>
<p>OpenAI points out that workflow benefits can go beyond saved time. Faster account research could matter because it improves preparation, but the business outcome might be a shorter sales cycle or a change in win rate.</p>
<p>That is why teams should record both process metrics and outcome metrics. Time saved is a workflow measure. Revenue, conversion or customer retention is a business measure. The second set can be influenced by many factors, so it should not be presented as an automatic result of AI usage.</p>
<h2>Be careful with vendor ROI claims</h2>
<p>OpenAI includes customer examples in its announcement, but it labels the figures as customer estimates or hypothetical where applicable. One example gives an estimate of 553% ROI for an engineering workflow using Codex. Another reports large reductions in time spent reviewing event listings and inventory tasks.</p>
<p>Those figures are useful case studies, not universal benchmarks. Results depend on the workflow, team, baseline, costs and how the calculation is defined. A good internal program should measure its own baseline rather than copy a vendor case-study percentage. For teams extending AI from measurement into production agents, the <a href="/chatgpt/openai-agents-api-production-guide">OpenAI Agents API production guide</a> covers the next layer of implementation and controls.</p>
<h2>How to use the Admin API</h2>
<p>OpenAI says teams can use the Admin API to automate reports in their own dashboards and combine AI usage analytics with business-system data. That can make the measurement more useful because the AI data can sit beside ticket resolution time, delivery data, CRM outcomes or other operational metrics.</p>
<p>A simple reporting table might contain: workflow, users, AI usage, human review time, baseline cycle time, current cycle time, quality measure and business outcome. This creates a repeatable review process without hiding the assumptions.</p>
<h2>Privacy and measurement boundaries</h2>
<p>OpenAI says it does not train its models on an organization’s business data by default. Teams should still review their own contractual, security and privacy requirements before exporting usage information into another analytics system.</p>
<p>Do not collect more user-level data than the business question requires. Aggregate views are often enough for team-level reporting, while individual-level data should have a clear operational purpose and access controls.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Is OpenAI’s ROI example a guaranteed result?</h3><p>No. Customer ROI figures are case-specific estimates and the announcement says some figures are hypothetical. Teams should build their own baseline and calculation.</p><h3>What should be measured first?</h3><p>Start with one common workflow tied to a business priority. Record the baseline and choose one or two outcome metrics that can be reviewed consistently.</p><h3>Can AI usage data be added to an existing dashboard?</h3><p>Yes. OpenAI says the Admin API can be used to automate reports and combine usage analytics with business-system data.</p>
